<!-- ============ COMPOSABLE POSITION ============ -->
<section class="section" style="padding-top: 0;">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="feature-row">
      <div class="feature-text">
        <span class="section-eyebrow">Where We Stand on Composable</span>
        <h2>Unified at the core. Composable at the edge.</h2>
        <p>Composable architecture solved a real problem: monolithic suites that forced a retailer to take every module from one vendor and wait years for any of them to change. It also created one, an estate of independently versioned services that agree on nothing, where every release is a regression cycle across a dozen contracts.</p>
        <p>Teamwork Commerce takes a deliberate position between the two. The transactional core (selling, inventory, orders, product, customer, identity) is one codebase with one data model, because those are the domains where disagreement between systems shows up as a wrong answer in front of a customer. Everything outside that core is yours to compose.</p>
        <ul class="feature-bullets">
          <li>One data model across POS, inventory, orders, product and customer, no sync window in which two systems disagree</li>
          <li>One release and one upgrade path, rather than a regression cycle per connected app</li>
          <li>Open APIs for import and export, so the core is readable and writable by the rest of your estate</li>
          <li>Custom services and logic overlays run alongside the core through external APIs, your bespoke workflow does not become a fork you are frozen on</li>
          <li>Interface composition per retailer: module sequence, order and exposure are configured, not coded</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="fine-note">If your target state mandates fully independent, separately deployable services for every retail domain, that is a genuine architectural difference worth testing early, bring it to a technical review rather than discovering it in an RFP response.</p>
      </div>
